---
/**
 * The homepage hero AS IT WAS BEFORE #27: the promise spanning the band, then two
 * columns — the words on solid midnight, the film beside them as a FRAMED screen,
 * square, with its 2px paper rule.
 *
 * ## Why this file exists
 *
 * The client preferred this one after seeing the full-bleed version live
 * (2026-08-27) and asked to compare them in two tabs. So it is restored verbatim
 * from `main` at d7e30ec — the last commit before #27 — rather than rebuilt from
 * memory, and it renders at `/hero-original/` beside the shipped hero at `/`.
 *
 * **This is a comparison surface, not a second homepage.** `/hero-original/` is
 * `noIndex` and out of the sitemap. When the client picks, one of these two
 * components and its page should be DELETED rather than left to rot — two heroes
 * sharing one body is fine for a week and a liability after that.
 *
 * ## What it does differently, in one place
 *
 * Nothing is composited in either hero, so neither pays FW-4003's 71% scrim. The
 * difference is what the film IS:
 *   - here it is an OBJECT — framed, square, its own 16:9, uncropped, and a
 *     visitor sees 100% of the frame
 *   - in `HeroBleed` it is a GROUND — full width, cover-cropped to whatever the
 *     panel's height needs (~66% of the frame survives at 1920), with the panel
 *     occluding part of what is left
 *
 * That is the actual trade the client is looking at: all of a smaller picture, or
 * some of a bigger one.
 */
import BookButton from '../venue/BookButton.astro';
import HeroFilm from '../venue/HeroFilm.astro';
import { buttonClasses } from '../../lib/buttons';
import lakevilleNightPoster from '../../assets/images/lakeville-night-poster.jpg';
---

{
  /* 1. HERO — the room at night, WITH THE SCREEN IN IT. Promise + what it is
      + where, one primary CTA. Venue-neutral by design: the venue question is
      asked at the Book click, not the front door. The subhead carries an 8px
      amber slab rather than an opacity — the block this replaced set its eyebrow
      at opacity-70 and its subhead at opacity-80, both of which re-derive their
      own contrast against whatever ground they land on.

      **The client's film is now the hero** (FW-4010), which FW-4003 declined —
      and the way it is declined matters, because the refusal was measured and
      the measurement was right. What it measured was ONE structure: display type
      composited over moving footage. So the film is here on a structure that
      composites nothing: the promise SPANS the grid, then the band opens into
      two columns — the words on solid midnight, the film beside them as a ruled
      screen. Contrast stops being a per-frame property and goes back to being a
      token pairing, and the film runs unscrimmed, which is the only version of
      it anyone actually wanted to look at. The re-measurement on the file that
      ships, and the two rejected structures, are in `HeroFilm`.

      The h1 spans both columns rather than sitting in one: `text-hero` peaks at
      5.25rem, which needs most of `max-w-content` to stay at two lines. A split
      that boxes it into half the width forces either five lines or a smaller
      step, and the page's single largest gesture is not the thing to trade away
      for a layout. Spanning it also makes the row beneath rhyme with the "Two
      venues" rows — media cell beside text cell on a ruled grid — rather than
      importing a video-hero composition from somewhere else.

      The left cell is `self-start` so the subhead's 8px amber slab starts on the
      same line as the film's top rule — measured in the browser at 1440, both at
      y=294 within the band. Bottom-aligning instead (tried, screenshotted,
      rejected) buys an alignment with the caption's last line, which is a weaker
      edge, and drops the primary CTA ~150px down the page to get it. The
      midnight left under the buttons is the field, not a gap.

      DOM order is eyebrow → h1 → words → film, on every viewport. On mobile that
      puts the film below the CTAs: an autoplaying clip above the promise buries
      both the h1 and the primary action on the device where the action matters
      most, and reordering it visually would split tab order from reading order
      around a focusable pause control. The film is still inside the hero band. */
}
<section class="on-dark bg-midnight">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter pt-s8 pb-s7 lg:px-gutter-lg lg:pt-s9 lg:pb-s8">
    <div class="grid gap-y-s5 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,5fr)_minmax(0,6fr)] lg:gap-x-s8">
      <p class="caps-label text-amber lg:col-span-2">Lakeville &amp; Stillwater, Minnesota</p>
      {
        /* THE CLIENT'S OWN HEADLINE, restored verbatim from their live homepage
            (`_ingest/raw/home.html`) and approved for reuse 2026-08-04 (FW-4000).
            Ours was "Indoor golf that's actually a night out." — which is fine, and
            theirs is better, because it is the sentence they already say about
            themselves. The subhead keeps the night-out job, so nothing is lost. */
      }
      <h1 class="text-hero leading-hero font-extrabold tracking-display-tight text-white lg:col-span-2">
        The most realistic indoor golf you can play, period.
      </h1>

      <div class="lg:self-start">
        <p class="max-w-prose-page border-l-slab border-amber pl-s4 text-lead leading-body text-paper">
          Ten GolfZon bays across two Twin Cities venues. Book by the hour, bring up to six people for the same price,
          and let the simulator keep score while you order another round.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-s6 flex flex-wrap gap-s4">
          <BookButton size="lg" variant="onDark" />
          <a href="/rates/" class={buttonClasses('lg', 'ghostDark')}>See rates &amp; hours</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <HeroFilm
        name="lakeville-night"
        poster={lakevilleNightPoster}
        alt="Silent video: a group of friends around a table with drinks and food in the Lakeville room, then a player stepping up and hitting a shot into a lit simulator screen."
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
